nsformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate down by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?"] [17592186079219 :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=4f(2x)-3,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}=0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(2x)$ by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=4f(2x)$ down by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x)-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3).$"] [17592186079219 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-fa5d3062-3532-5d26-b4cb-17046684e70f" :answer/id #uuid "fa5d3062-3532-5d26-b4cb-17046684e70f"] ["answer-fa5d3062-3532-5d26-b4cb-17046684e70f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-fa5d3062-3532-5d26-b4cb-17046684e70f" :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)"] ["answer-c2f27536-7f52-5f15-81d2-99bca9257fa6" :answer/id #uuid "c2f27536-7f52-5f15-81d2-99bca9257fa6"] ["answer-c2f27536-7f52-5f15-81d2-99bca9257fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c2f27536-7f52-5f15-81d2-99bca9257fa6" :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1)"] ["answer-3df56a22-be89-53fc-a186-949e5208a495" :answer/id #uuid "3df56a22-be89-53fc-a186-949e5208a495"] ["answer-3df56a22-be89-53fc-a186-949e5208a495" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3df56a22-be89-53fc-a186-949e5208a495" :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)"] ["answer-e587188b-268f-535d-aee2-c34d70d9f812" :answer/id #uuid "e587188b-268f-535d-aee2-c34d70d9f812"] ["answer-e587188b-268f-535d-aee2-c34d70d9f812" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e587188b-268f-535d-aee2-c34d70d9f812" :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1)"] ["answer-16989c60-1053-533b-9f14-b9ea3d24df0c" :answer/id #uuid "16989c60-1053-533b-9f14-b9ea3d24df0c"] ["answer-16989c60-1053-533b-9f14-b9ea3d24df0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-16989c60-1053-533b-9f14-b9ea3d24df0c" :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)"] [17592186078092 :answer-field/correct "answer-16989c60-1053-533b-9f14-b9ea3d24df0c"] [17592186078092 :answer-field/choices "answer-fa5d3062-3532-5d26-b4cb-17046684e70f"] [17592186078092 :answer-field/choices "answer-c2f27536-7f52-5f15-81d2-99bca9257fa6"] [17592186078092 :answer-field/choices "answer-3df56a22-be89-53fc-a186-949e5208a495"] [17592186078092 :answer-field/choices "answer-e587188b-268f-535d-aee2-c34d70d9f812"] [17592186078092 :answer-field/choices "answer-16989c60-1053-533b-9f14-b9ea3d24df0c"] ["question-a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f" :question/id #uuid "a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f"] ["question-a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112955"] ["question-a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f" :question/problem "The graph of $y=3f(5x-15),$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.2$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Translate right by $3$ units\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?"] ["question-a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=3f(5x-15)=3f(5(x-3))\n$$\n\nTo get $y=3f(5(x-3)),$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{5}=0.2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(5x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Translate $y=f(5x)$ right by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(5(x-3)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Stretch $y=f(5(x-3))$ by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=3f(5(x-3)).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1).$"] ["question-a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-1db9c8b6-c498-58eb-a73c-ab6c2a805e78" :answer-field/id #uuid "1db9c8b6-c498-58eb-a73c-ab6c2a805e78"] ["answer-c075a111-f868-5188-9ba9-3a5f5292a779" :answer/id #uuid "c075a111-f868-5188-9ba9-3a5f5292a779"] ["answer-c075a111-f868-5188-9ba9-3a5f5292a779" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c075a111-f868-5188-9ba9-3a5f5292a779" :answer/value